#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# airy_ai

#% text
# `airy_ai(x)` gives the Airy function `Ai(x)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `Ai(x)` is the solution of `y'' = x y` that decays as `x` tends to `inf` (DLMF 9.2); it oscillates for negative `x`.

#% item
# `airy_ai(0)` is `3^(-2/3)/gamma(2/3)`. Machine numbers, balls and complex numbers give numerical values.

#% item
# `airy_ai` is listable.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
airy_ai(0)
#% output
#= 3^(1/3)/(3*gamma(2/3))

#% cell
airy_ai(1.0)
#% output
#= 0.13529241631288141

#% cell
airy_ai(-5.0)
#% output
#= 0.35076100902411433

#% cell
n(airy_ai(1), 30)
#% output
#= 0.1352924163128814155241474235154663

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Fast decay:

#% cell
airy_ai(20.0)
#% output
#= 1.6916728686705404e-27

#% text
# A complex argument:

#% cell
airy_ai(1.0 + 1.0*im)
#% output
#= 0.06045830837183816 - 0.15188956587718147im

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The first zero, which sets the lowest energy of a particle in a linear potential:

#% cell
find_root(airy_ai(x) == 0, [x, -2.3])
#% output
#= [x -> -2.338107410459767]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The derivative is `airy_ai_prime`:

#% cell
d(airy_ai(x), x)
#% output
#= airy_ai_prime(x)

#% text
# The differential equation:

#% cell
d(airy_ai(x), [x, 2]) - x*airy_ai(x)
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `airy_bi`, `airy_ai_prime`, `airy_bi_prime`, `bessel_k`
